WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.960
So just one day after we got our hands on
these extraordinarily incriminating

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.770
suggesting that Donald Trump's lawyers henchman
I'm talking of course of Rudy Giuliani's henchman,

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.950
Lev Parness and Igor Fruman.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:21.749
Uh, after the texts exposed that they had
ambassador Maria Bonovitch under physical

00:00:21.749 --> 00:00:26.660
surveillance, Nancy Pelosi and the house of
representatives have moved forward with the

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:32.200
impeachment of Donald Trump sending the two
articles of impeachment over to the United

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.200
States Senate.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.860
The vote to do this was almost among party
lines or along party lines, I guess I would

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.270
say two 28 to one 93.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:47.610
The only Democrat Colin Peterson, uh, to vote
a no was a guy who is known as a Democrat

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:48.810
in name only.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:49.810
Collin Peterson.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:55.870
Frequently grandstanding with Republicans
despite being a Democrat in some technical

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:56.870
sense.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.210
Uh, the house also voted to approve seven
impeachment managers.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:05.299
Now these, these are all Democrats of course,
who are effectively going to act as sort of

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:10.039
prosecuting attorneys against Donald Trump
in the Senate trial.

00:01:10.039 --> 00:01:11.090
This will be explosive.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:12.090
It's going to be ugly.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.840
We're going to talk about the proceedings
and what we can expect a little bit later.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.520
The seven impeachment managers that have been
selected are Adam Schiff, Hakeem Jeffries,

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:28.939
Jason Crow, Jerry Nadler, Sylvia Garcia, Val
Demings, and Zoe Lofgren.

00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:33.380
Maybe the most interesting inclusion in that
list is Jason Crow from Colorado.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.789
He's a first term Congressman.

00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:40.740
He's not on the house judiciary or intelligence
committees from which every other impeachment

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.530
manager hails.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:47.359
But the theme here is that everybody who is
going to be all seven of these individuals

00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:52.369
have, uh, either law degrees or law enforcement
backgrounds.

00:01:52.369 --> 00:01:59.270
Interestingly, Zoe Lofgren has served in all
modern impeachment related proceedings.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:04.020
She was a staffer during the Nixon pre impeachment,
which never became an impeachment.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.939
It ended up with his resignation, Nixon's
resignation.

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:11.420
Once it became clear that impeachment would
likely lead to a conviction because Republican

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.940
voters and Republican senators had abandoned
him.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:21.120
Uh, she was on the judiciary committees, Zoe
Lofgren during bill Clinton's impeachment,

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.700
and here she is again, uh, during Donald Trump's
impeachment.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:30.250
The trial is tentatively scheduled to start
Tuesday, but there is going to be a lot of

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:35.690
lead up before what you and I would consider
the trial actually gets going.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.820
I'll talk that a little bit later.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:43.470
We're still trying to figure out what will
be public, what might they push behind closed

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:44.470
doors.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:48.110
When we know more, we'll know more about what
we will or won't be.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.280
Live streaming.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:55.580
My instinct is to stream as much of what is
public as possible and we'll talk about that

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.680
a little bit later on in the show as well.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.130
There are still questions about what witnesses
are going to be there.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:06.720
Remember that while you need two thirds of
the Senate to convict Trump, uh, 67 senators

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.490
effectively, which would be 45 Democrats,
the two independents that caucus with Democrats

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:19.250
plus 20 Republicans, in order to convict Donald
Trump for subpoenaing witnesses, for agreeing

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:25.210
to subpoena witnesses, you only need 51 you
only need a simple majority and that means

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.380
you only need four Republicans.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:32.930
Assuming all 47 Democrats and independents
are onboard, only four Republicans need to

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.460
agree to subpoena a witness.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.540
Someone in order to make that happen.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:42.060
We've heard that Mitt Romney wants to hear
from John Bolton.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.150
So is does that mean he would vote to subpoena
him?

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.460
That's one vote that you would still need
three more.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.240
And then of course the normal names are always
floated.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:54.960
Maybe Lisa Murkowski, although maybe not,
maybe Susan Collins, although often not.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.819
Um, maybe Cory gardener, although we can't
really say the same sort of general questions

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:05.060
apply to which documents are going to be allowed.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:11.650
Certainly the absolutely insane text messages
about Maria Bonovitch being under physical

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:16.959
surveillance, watching her movements and relaying
them back to left Parness that should be part

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:21.320
of the trial Senate majority leader, Mitch
McConnell is absolutely going to try to keep

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.180
those documents out.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:28.200
And of course, Donald Trump's immediate reaction
was to start tweeting wacky stuff, which we're

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.030
going to look at in a moment.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.680
I think this is a good time to remember.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:39.660
We want to be evidence-based and when we look
at the evidence, we shouldn't irrationally

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:46.160
and, uh, irrationally optimistically believe
that this is a process that's going to lead

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.070
to Donald Trump's conviction.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:52.780
I know that I still get emails from people
saying, David, don't assume as a foregone

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.630
conclusion that Donald Trump will be acquitted
in the Senate.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:56.630
Okay.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.110
With 99% certainty.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:03.210
I, I've been reading a great book called thinking
in bets, which says we should, we should talk

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:10.910
more about confidence with 99% certainty Donald
Trump is not going to be convicted in this

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:17.930
trial, so therefore I'm going to try to focus
my analysis of the trial on the basis that

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:24.430
given that a conviction is so unlikely, what
is the best setup for ultimately helping to

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:29.420
push Trump out of the oval office by voting
in November of 2020 ultimately removing him

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:35.990
in January of 2021 given that it is unlikely
that a conviction is at the tail end of this,

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.570
what can Democrats do during the trial?

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:43.760
How can they manage the obstructive desires
of Mitch McConnell and other Republicans in

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:47.810
the lead up to the trial to most damaged Donald
Trump?

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:51.260
That's the lens I think is the most pragmatic
one.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:57.040
If there are still people in my audience who
truly believe that the outcome of this trial,

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.790
that is it, we now know it's not at some vague
point in the future.

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:01.790
It starts Tuesday.

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:03.140
Unless something changes.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:07.180
If I still have people in my audience who
believe there could be a conviction coming

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:10.460
at the tail end of this, I want to hear from
you.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:15.960
I mean, I w I want to hear which 20 Republicans
do you believe might vote to convict or if

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.660
you believe that it's not about finding 20
Republicans, but it's about something in general

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:25.360
will happen in the next couple of weeks that
will turn the entire Republican party against

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:26.360
Donald Trump.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.830
I'm talking about his supporters, uh, because
that's what it would take to get Republicans

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:31.300
to abandon him.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:35.120
Get in touch and explain to me exactly how
